  <ym-title|Review of bounded coarse approximations><label|review-of-bounded-coarse-approximations>

  Source: Scott <verbatim|part2/E-bounded-real.tex>; suppliers: Output
  Chapters 4 and 11. This records conditions for the new Chapter 13 supplier,
  not a review of a completed fragment.

  <\enumerate>
    <item><strong|Conditional factorization requires full random-seed cones.>
    The actual block kernel is a mixture over shared frame choices, not a
    product in coarse links conditional only on the fine field. Include the
    independent frame seeds, predictor seeds, and link-noise seeds, with
    every fine-link read and eligibility test, in each ancestor cone.
    Disjoint such cones give conditional independence given the terminal fine
    field; induction over the composed kernels proves the required product
    pullback identity. Merely disjoint coarse links do not suffice. A kernel
    patch has radius of order the convolution order times the next coarse
    spacing; sum these radii over the ancestor levels when proving the cell
    margin. The selected schedule must make this total radius tend to zero
    with the stopping mesh.

    <item><strong|Use the actual terminal charge in graph inversion.> Scott
    bounds the terminal residual by <verbatim|2(1+C_h)> as if the trial had
    unit charge. The current general trial theorem gives
    <verbatim|2(1+C_h)Q_h>. Use the stronger last-step endpoint
  </enumerate>

  <verbatim|\|e_j-J_h e_j\| \<less\>= 2(1+C_h)Q_h L_(h-1)^(-1/8)>

  and purchase a finite pre-length stage floor making this at most 1/4. The
  guarded coarse polynomial has identity readout because its guard is
  identically one on the ordinary local chart. Its exact terminal
  representation must still retain the old selected large-field inventories.
  The normalized square-root allowance attachment estimate from the corrected
  equation calibration can make Q_h a stage-controlled amount; the larger
  unweighted reserve belongs separately in E_h. Establish that the guard has
  a strict plateau on the chart and that its full test stencil is used in the
  cone.

  <\enumerate>
    <assign|item-nr|2><item><strong|Same history and bounds.> Apply the
    terminal comparison to the effective density at h belonging to the actual
    history from N. Do not replace it by an independent Wilson terminal law.
    The matrix J_h and the recombination coefficients may depend on N,p.
    Their inverse and the finite product of reference-to-h column bounds must
    be uniform in N,p. Then the bounded polynomial at fixed h, the
    probability-kernel supremum contraction, and the recombination
    coefficient bound give a finite cutoff/volume-independent supremum. The
    root-error charge E_h must be bounded uniformly in N,p and grow subpower
    in a_h^{-1}; it need not be bounded uniformly in h.

    <item><strong|Replicate before marginalizing.> A single-column root
    comparison is not by itself an all-copy signed-gas bound. Insert all
    signed marks before integrating, inject the coarse factors at h, and
    retain all joint modes transported from N to h. Disjoint replica cones
    confine principal connected components to one replica cell. Multicell
    modes require the stronger support exponent and the higher-mode induction
    of Chapter 11; their support reserve pays the increased residual weights.
    The replicated density identity and vacuum ratio can then give an error
    of the form <verbatim|C E_h product(column bounds)
    exp(-(tau_h-tau_m)/8)>. The subpower factor is dominated by this decay.
    This is the quantitative justification needed for the bounded
    approximation, beyond finite-dimensional graph inversion.

    <item><strong|Real words.> Once the single-field centered-seminorm
    estimate is established, Scott's word argument is sound: take real parts,
    telescope products in distinct cells, bound by <verbatim|n delta
    (C+delta)^(n-1)>, and apply this to the four products in the two
    reflected-word differences. The large supremum bound never enters this
    replacement error. Ordered tails may depend on each fixed translated pair
    and their separation; a common cutoff threshold for all translation
    parameters is not asserted or needed. Set approximants to zero off their
    admissible tails to obtain the global supremum statement.
  </enumerate>

  These requirements and the two concrete corrections (shared frame seeds;
  nonunit terminal charge) were sent to the parent before drafting the
  supplier.

  <section|Review of the added Chapter 13
  fragment><label|review-of-the-added-chapter-13-fragment>

  The fragment explicitly retains the coarse-matrix and replicated-residual
  estimates as hypotheses, so it does not hide the remaining all-copy
  payment. Added the authorized cone-radius paragraph using the actual
  Chapter 4 radius <verbatim|(j_i+3)L_i>, endpoint displacement, and boundary
  margins. The resulting bound is <verbatim|r_d a_h +
  sum_{i\<gtr\>=h}(j_i+7)a_i>. A finite stage-entry request gives
  <verbatim|(j_i+7)\<less\>=a_i^(-1/2)> and hence a uniform tail bounded by
  <verbatim|sqrt(a_h)/(1-3^(-1/2))>. The paragraph includes this request and
  explains why it fits the existing diagonal schedule. Reported the separate
  block-floor wording correction: the eighth root of the block length, not
  the length itself, must dominate <verbatim|8(1+C_h)Q_h>.

  Brief assembly check: Chapter 19 keeps the remaining analytic comparisons
  conditional, and separates the OPE supplier from the unconditional-on-OPE
  contact extension. Chapter 18 now chooses one subtraction per invariant
  graded tuple and its permutations, then extends by finite multilinearity.
  This supplies the claimed filtration compatibility and covariance; the
  stated absence of automatic unit deletion/contact Ward normalization
  remains correct.

  <section|Final residual supplier review><label|final-residual-supplier-review>

  Reviewed the new <verbatim|ch13:coarse-residual-supplier> proof. Physical
  normalization is correct: the adjacent ratio of a_i^(-d)/G(h,i) is
  L_i^(d+1/8), paid by the chosen L_i^(d+1) height recursion. At h the
  negative primary costs at most 1+\|x_h\|\<less\>=2E_h; the same weight pays
  its service above h. The local attachment height uses polynomial inverse
  u_i=4 sqrt(beta_i), not the inverse exponentially small large-field
  allowance. Joint modes above and below h remain in the full source
  polynomial, whose subset coefficient identity is exactly the product of
  pullback discrepancies under disjoint full seed cones.

  Requested and verified two corrections. First, reweight on incoming fine
  support, with B_i\<less\>=B_(i+1). Maintain a fixed full exponent A_i at
  each level and impose 10<ym-middot>15^4(A_i+9)\<less\>=L_i(A_(i+1)\<minus\>4):
  extracting four fine reserve units and using cover contraction recovers the
  full coarse norm. Thus the proof does not spend the same finite reserve
  anew without renewal on infinitely many steps. Second, the positive trial's
  scalar is a global unit/exponential entry; only its guarded nonconstant
  part has zero complementary profile. The corrected proof states both
  points. No further concrete issue found.
